I’ll trace the clocked assignments and initialization first, then check which invariants make the property 1-inductive for the RTL as written.For the RTL as written, the target holds vacuously: cache A never reaches `S`. The final `if (update) bus_req <= 0; else bus_req <= bus_req;` overrides every earlier nonblocking assignment to `bus_req` in the same process. Both requests therefore remain zero, keeping the arbiter idle.

With the arbiter idle, each cache executes `next_state <= state` and `state <= next_state`. Because these are nonblocking assignments, the initialized pair `(I, D)` alternates with `(D, I)`.

The following lemmas are jointly 1-inductive and make the target 1-inductive. Use `@(posedge clk)` when asserting the target as well.

